AHS-017 — Event Model

Document ID: AHS-017
Title: Event Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

Architectural Views: Process, Information, Implementation

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical Event Model of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Event Model establishes how engineering events are represented, published, propagated, correlated, and governed throughout Atlas while preserving engineering semantics, traceability, and temporal consistency.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Event concepts
Event taxonomy
Event lifecycle
Event metadata
Event ordering
Event correlation
Event governance

This specification does not define event brokers, streaming platforms, or transport technologies.

3. Canonical Definition

An Engineering Event is an immutable record that announces the occurrence of a meaningful change in engineering reality, cognition, workflow, or system state.

Events describe facts that have occurred.

Events SHALL NOT request actions.

4. Event Objectives

Atlas events SHALL:

Represent completed occurrences.
Be immutable after publication.
Preserve engineering semantics.
Preserve temporal ordering where required.
Preserve traceability.
Support distributed cognition.
Enable reactive workflows.
5. Event Principles

Every Engineering Event SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Immutability

Published events SHALL NOT be modified.

Fact-Based

Events SHALL represent facts rather than intentions.

Traceability

Every event SHALL reference its originating engineering artifacts.

Temporal Integrity

Events SHALL accurately represent when the occurrence took place.

Loose Coupling

Event producers SHALL remain independent of consumers.

Replayability

Events MAY be replayed without changing their original meaning.

6. Event Taxonomy

Atlas defines six canonical event categories.

6.1 Observation Events

Examples:

Sensor reading captured
Drawing uploaded
Site inspection completed
User input received
6.2 Cognitive Events

Examples:

Understanding created
Knowledge updated
Reasoning completed
Confidence recalculated
6.3 Workflow Events

Examples:

Workflow started
Task assigned
Workflow paused
Workflow completed
6.4 Decision Events

Examples:

Recommendation generated
Decision approved
Decision rejected
Decision revised
6.5 Governance Events

Examples:

Policy updated
Constraint violated
Compliance verified
Audit completed
6.6 Platform Events

Examples:

Service started
Configuration changed
Interface published
Deployment completed
7. Canonical Event Structure

Every Engineering Event SHALL contain:

Element	Description
Event ID	Globally unique identifier
Event Type	Canonical event classification
Event Name	Human-readable name
Timestamp	Occurrence time
Producer	Originating participant
Subject	Affected artifact or entity
Payload	Relevant Canonical Engineering Artifact(s)
Context	Engineering context
Correlation ID	Links related events
Causation ID	References the event that triggered this event
Version	Event schema version
Traceability References	Links to originating artifacts and decisions
8. Event Lifecycle
Detected
    │
Validated
    │
Published
    │
Distributed
    │
Consumed
    │
Processed
    │
Archived

Published events SHALL remain immutable throughout their lifecycle.

9. Event Ordering

Atlas recognizes three ordering models.

Strict Ordering

Events SHALL be processed exactly in occurrence order.

Example:

Structural load monitoring.

Partition Ordering

Ordering guaranteed within a logical partition.

Example:

One construction project.

Unordered

Ordering is not required.

Example:

Independent notifications.

Ordering requirements SHALL be defined by the event contract.

10. Event Correlation

Related events SHALL be correlated using:

Correlation ID
Workflow ID
Decision ID
Artifact ID
Project ID

Correlation enables complete reconstruction of engineering activities.

11. Event Causality

Every event MAY reference the event that caused it.

Example:

Site Inspection Completed
           │
           ▼
Evidence Validated
           │
           ▼
Reasoning Started
           │
           ▼
Recommendation Generated

Causation SHALL preserve the engineering reasoning chain.

12. Event Governance

Every event SHALL define:

Ownership
Publication authority
Retention policy
Security classification
Privacy requirements
Replay policy
Version compatibility

Governance SHALL ensure trustworthy event management.

13. Event Quality Attributes

Engineering events SHALL be evaluated for:

Accuracy
Completeness
Timeliness
Consistency
Integrity
Traceability
Replayability
Observability
14. Conformance

An implementation conforms if it:

Uses canonical event structures.
Preserves event immutability.
Supports event correlation.
Maintains event traceability.
Documents ordering guarantees.
15. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Semantic event routing.
Event prioritization.
Distributed event federation.
Cross-domain event interoperability.
Autonomous event synthesis.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical event semantics.